<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuchihri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1040</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Manuchehri Damghani, Manuchihri Damghani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuchihri was a Persian poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a> who served at the court of the Ghaznavid sultan Mas'ud I. He is celebrated for his lively descriptions of nature, wine and the seasons, and is credited with developing the musammat, a strophic verse form. He is counted, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unsuri">Unsuri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhi-sistani">Farrukhi</a>, among the leading poets of the Ghaznavid court.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the words of the scholar J. W. Clinton, very little is known of the poet's life, and that little comes only from his poetry; later tadhkira writers, the compilers of biographies of poets, expanded it with speculations that are easily refuted. His epithet Damghani shows that he came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. His verse displays a wide knowledge of Arabic and Persian poetry, which he presumably acquired in youth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His career can be dated only through the people his praise poems address. About a third of his panegyrics are addressed to the sultan, and most of the rest to leading officials of the sultan's court, though some name patrons who cannot be identified or none at all. In 1031–1033 he wrote poems for the sultan's deputies while the ruler was based at Ray.</p><p>Some time after the death of the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hasan-maymandi">Ahmad ibn Hasan Maymandi</a> in 1033 he made his way to the court at Ghazna. None of his poems seems to date from after his time there; the sultan died in 1041 after his defeat in battle at Dandanaqan. Clinton counts him the third and last, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unsuri">Unsuri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhi-sistani">Farrukhi</a>, of the major panegyrists of the early court of Ghazna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His divan holds fifty-seven qasidas, a few ruba'is, ghazals and shorter pieces, and eleven musammats, stanza poems of a form he is said to have invented in Persian. Clinton sets him apart from his contemporaries by his enthusiasm for Arabic poetry, which led him to imitate the old Arabic qasida, and by his skill in describing the royal garden at Nawruz and Mihrgan and its festivities. He also gave life to ideas such as the battle of the seasons and wine as the daughter of the vine.</p><p>One of his best known musammats, of 35 stanzas, begins by calling on the listener to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> because autumn has come and a cold wind blows from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, and plays on words of similar sound. His text with a translation and notes was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1886 by A. de Biberstein Kazimirski. From 1939 the British modernist poet Basil Bunting published adaptations of several of his poems, and something of their sound-patterning seems to have passed into Bunting's own verse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuchehri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Persian was the language of court poetry and much of the administration, and Ghazni drew poets such as Unsuri, Farrukhi and <b>Manuchihri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Semnan Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.23,53.92&amp;year=1030" aria-label="Semnan Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuchihri"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4576 4542h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4576 4542h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.23,53.92&amp;year=1030">What was here in 1030? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
